# No types / constants / enums in services

Service files contain **business logic only**. Declaring `type`, `interface`, `enum`, or module-level `const` in a `*.service.ts` file is forbidden.

## Required locations

| Kind | Put it here |
|---|---|
| `type` / `interface` | `<feature>/types/<feature>.types.ts` |
| `enum` | `<feature>/enum/<feature>.enums.ts` (or existing enum file) |
| Constants / default objects | `<feature>/constants/<feature>.constants.ts` |
| Pure transforms of those types | `<feature>/helpers/*.helper.ts` |

## Wrong

```ts
// ❌ foo.service.ts
type ProjectScope = { organizationId: string; projectId: string; userId: string };
const LIST_LIMIT = 4;
enum Mode { A = 'A' }

@Injectable()
export class FooService { /* ... */ }
```

## Right

```ts
// types/foo.types.ts
export type ProjectScope = { organizationId: string; projectId: string; userId: string };

// constants/foo.constants.ts
export const LIST_LIMIT = 4;

// foo.service.ts
import { LIST_LIMIT } from '../constants/foo.constants';
import type { ProjectScope } from '../types/foo.types';
```

## Hard rules

- Do **not** export types from a service for other modules to import. Export from `types/` instead.
- Do **not** leave file-local `type` aliases at the top of a service "just for this file".
- Props-like input shapes for private methods still belong in `types/`.
- Specs may keep local fixtures; production `service/*.ts` may not.
- When touching a service that still has inline types/constants/enums, **move them in the same change** before adding logic.

This rule overrides any looser wording elsewhere. Prefer this file when in conflict with older examples that showed inline types in services.
